{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"# Hand-built LLM sub-calls bypass the model-class config and go to direct Anthropic\n\n## Scope: FOUR sites, one root cause, THREE take the class fix\n\nEach builds a `RunRequest` by hand with `config=cfg` and declares no model class, so it inherits\n`cfg.model` regardless of how the three model classes are configured. MEASURED, not inferred — see\nthe comments for the full experiment:\n\n    llm/overlap/judge.py:46              op=overlap-judge    18 calls   <-- 18 of 23 in a plan review\n    llm/enrich.py:81                     op=ticket-digest     1 call    <-- runs on the STORE WRITE path\n    llm/operations.py:177                op=ticket-quality    1 call\n    llm/code_review/workflow_ops.py:571  op=code-novelty      1 call    <-- the site originally filed\n\nEverything else honours the configuration: all four plan-review passes, the completion verifier, and\nall seven code-review workflow steps.\n\n## Why this matters beyond consistency\n\nBedrock is normally chosen for data-residency, procurement or contractual reasons. These calls send\nreal content to direct Anthropic: the diff and prior findings (`code-novelty`), ticket text\n(`ticket-digest`, `ticket-quality`), and ticket digests for pairwise comparison (`overlap-judge`).\n\n**`overlap-judge` dominates plan-review traffic** — 18 of 23 calls. An operator who cuts over by\nconfiguring the three classes (the documented mechanism, and the one story eb6e will use) leaves the\nMAJORITY of plan-review traffic on direct Anthropic.\n\n**`ticket-digest` is the most insidious** because it is not in a gate at all: `enrich_drain.maybe_drain`\nis called from `_store/event_append.py:609` and `_store/push.py:295`, so it runs on the ticket STORE\nWRITE path — on ordinary `rebar comment` / `rebar create` calls, with no gate invocation and nothing\nin any verdict to reveal it.\n\n## The two configurations, and why both are needed\n\n* classes on Bedrock, `cfg.model` left bare-anthropic → PRE-FIX, all four sites above show `anthropic`: they\n  bypass the class config. This is the defect.\n* classes AND `cfg.model` on Bedrock → 15 calls, 0 on anthropic: nothing is HARDCODED past config.\n\nThe second configuration passes even with this bug unfixed, because a bypassing call inherits\n`cfg.model`. Any regression test must therefore pin the FIRST configuration, or it proves nothing.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- [ ] with a model-class table configured to values that DIFFER from `cfg.model`, no hand-built\n      SUB-CALL in review-plan, review-code or verify-completion resolves to `cfg.model` — verified\n      IN-SESSION by `tests/unit/test_subcall_class_selection.py`, which configures exactly such a\n      table (the only configuration in which \"honoured the class\" and \"fell through to cfg.model\"\n      are distinguishable strings — with no table configured both resolve to the same default and\n      the observation carries no information) and asserts, per site, the model that reaches the\n      runner. The two TOP-LEVEL one-call ops (`operations.py::review_ticket`,\n      `spec_scan.py::_scan_epics_inner`) are EXCLUDED by design and registered in\n      `_CFG_MODEL_BY_DESIGN`. Reframed from \"asserted from a live run's usage log\": the property is\n      a durable invariant of the code, so it belongs in a test that fails the day a new bypassing\n      sub-call is written, not in a one-off credentialed run that cannot be re-checked.\n- [ ] each of the THREE hand-built sub-call sites selects its model from the class\n      vocabulary, and the class chosen for each is stated in the code with its reason rather\n      than left implicit: `enrich.py` (ticket-digest) -> trivial, `overlap/judge.py`\n      (overlap-judge) -> standard, `code_review/workflow_ops.py::score_code_novelty`\n      (code-novelty) -> standard. The fourth site originally in scope,\n      `operations.py::review_ticket`, is DELIBERATELY left on `cfg.model` and registered in\n      `_CFG_MODEL_BY_DESIGN` with that reason: it is a top-level op's single LLM call, so\n      there are no passes to differentiate and the operator's configured model is the right\n      knob. Narrowed from \"four sites\" after the operator decision that one-call top-level\n      ops keep `cfg.model`; the code was already correct and the criterion was stale.\n- [ ] `code-novelty`'s choice is consistent with plan-review's novelty sub-call, or the difference is\n      documented as deliberate at both sites — `score_code_novelty`'s docstring currently claims it\n      \"mirrors\" `_score_floor_novelty`, which uses `_verifier_cfg`, and that parity does not hold\n- [ ] a test fails if ANY hand-built sub-call inherits `cfg.model` while a class table is configured\n      — the general defect, so a future hand-built sub-call cannot silently reintroduce it. A test\n      that enumerates today's four sites by name does NOT satisfy this\n- [ ] the `overlap-judge` test constructs its trigger deliberately: `wire.py:overlap_findings` runs\n      the judge only when BM25F retrieval returns candidates (`if not candidates: return []`, with\n      the query's own graph excluded), so a test that merely runs a gate and hopes the judge fires\n      will silently pass while testing nothing — both probes during the investigation hit exactly\n      that and produced zero judge calls\n- [ ] `ticket-digest`'s fix holds on the STORE WRITE path, not only inside a gate run\n- [ ] fail-safe behaviour is preserved at every site: the novelty signal must still degrade to\n      \"keep more findings\", and the overlap step must remain advisory and never block a review\n\n## Not in scope\n\nSibling `03b0` (a configured Bedrock provider is dropped for colon-bearing model ids) is a different\nseam — that one breaks the configuration before it is read; this one ignores it after."}},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1785696724281726001,"uuid":"da0e4e8c-eced-4b65-913f-761fbbfc6d5e"}